<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of West Florida</h1><p class="st-meta">1810 CE – 1810 CE · capital St. Francisville</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A 74-day republic declared by American settlers in Spanish West Florida in 1810, swiftly annexed by the United States.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The strip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-coast-of-the-united-states">Gulf Coast</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdido-river">Perdido</a> rivers remained Spanish after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a>, though the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> claimed it. Many settlers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baton-rouge">Baton Rouge</a> district were Americans. On 23 September 1810 rebels stormed the Spanish fort at Baton Rouge, and three days later a convention declared independence.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic adopted a constitution modelled on American practice, elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulwar-skipwith">Fulwar Skipwith</a> as governor and set its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-francisville-louisiana">St. Francisville</a>. It flew a blue flag with a single white star, later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonnie-blue-flag">Bonnie Blue Flag</a>. Its leaders hoped for admission to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> on favourable terms, and it raised a small militia that tried to march on Mobile.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> proclaimed the territory part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> in October 1810, and American troops took over in December. Most of it became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida-parishes">Florida Parishes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>; the area east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pearl-river-mississippi-louisiana">Pearl River</a> later went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-west-florida.jpg" alt="Republic of West Florida" width="320" height="230"><figcaption>Image: Wolfmaster2 for original raster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_Florida_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1810 CE – 1810 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>St. Francisville</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fulwar Skipwith</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>West Florida Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1810&amp;focus=republic-of-west-florida" aria-label="Republic of West Florida on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2314 1055 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of West Florida lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2634 1311L2886 1342M2634 1311L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2886 1342h0M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2634 1311h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2634 1311h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks St. Francisville. <a href="/atlas?year=1810&amp;focus=republic-of-west-florida">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
